import{_ as a,I as s,c as n,o as i,ag as t,J as o}from"./chunks/framework.Vyheyqg9.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Trees, graphs and traversal","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"cs-foundations/trees-graphs-traversal.md","filePath":"cs-foundations/trees-graphs-traversal.md"}'),d={name:"cs-foundations/trees-graphs-traversal.md"};function c(l,e,h,p,u,g){const r=s("MermaidDiagram");return i(),n("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=t('<h1 id="trees-graphs-and-traversal" tabindex="-1">Trees, graphs and traversal <a class="header-anchor" href="#trees-graphs-and-traversal" aria-label="Permalink to “Trees, graphs and traversal”">​</a></h1><p>A traversal must define <strong>what is reachable</strong>, what order matters and what result it promises. A visited set ensures “once per vertex”; it does not by itself prove acyclicity, dependency order or shortest weighted paths.</p><h2 id="represent-the-problem" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">1 </span>Represent the problem <a class="header-anchor" href="#represent-the-problem" aria-label="Permalink to “1 Represent the problem”">​</a></h2><p>Our directed graph is <code>Map&lt;string, readonly string[]&gt;</code>: each vertex has an ordered neighbor list. All referenced vertices must exist; duplicate adjacency entries reject. Cycles and self-loops are valid. Bounds are 200 vertices, 2,000 directed neighbor entries, and IDs of 1–40 ASCII letters/digits/underscore/hyphen. This is a small owned teaching graph, not a parser for arbitrary JSON or a global dependency service.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Representation</th><th>Storage</th><th>Useful operation / cost</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adjacency list</td><td>O(V+E) entries</td><td>Iterate one vertex&#39;s actual neighbors; edge membership may scan degree</td></tr><tr><td>Adjacency matrix</td><td>O(V²) cells</td><td>O(1) indexed edge check; enumerate a row in O(V)</td></tr><tr><td>Edge list</td><td>O(V+E) including explicit isolated vertices</td><td>Simple import/export; finding neighbors without an index scans edges</td></tr></tbody></table><p>An undirected edge usually needs two adjacency entries; count both. Vertex identity and missing-vertex policy are part of the contract. Traversing from one start visits only its reachable component, even when validation examines the whole graph.</p><h2 id="worked-cyclic-and-disconnected-graph" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">2 </span>Worked cyclic and disconnected graph <a class="header-anchor" href="#worked-cyclic-and-disconnected-graph" aria-label="Permalink to “2 Worked cyclic and disconnected graph”">​</a></h2>',7)),o(r,{"encoded-source":"flowchart%20LR%0A%20%20%20%20a%20--%3E%20b%0A%20%20%20%20a%20--%3E%20c%0A%20%20%20%20b%20--%3E%20d%0A%20%20%20%20c%20--%3E%20d%0A%20%20%20%20d%20--%3E%20a%0A%20%20%20%20z%5Bisolated%20z%5D"}),e[1]||(e[1]=t('<p>With neighbor order <code>a:[b,c], b:[d], c:[d], d:[a], z:[]</code>:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Traversal from a</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>BFS discovery/processing order</td><td>a, b, c, d</td></tr><tr><td>BFS hop distances</td><td>a:0, b:1, c:1, d:2</td></tr><tr><td>DFS preorder</td><td>a, b, d, c</td></tr><tr><td>DFS postorder</td><td>d, b, c, a</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Neither visits z. Changing neighbor order can change ties/preorder/postorder without changing reachable sets or shortest hop distances.</p><h2 id="bfs-distance-invariant" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">3 </span>BFS distance invariant <a class="header-anchor" href="#bfs-distance-invariant" aria-label="Permalink to “3 BFS distance invariant”">​</a></h2><p>BFS uses a FIFO queue and records distance when <strong>enqueuing</strong>, not when dequeuing. Initially only the start is known at distance zero. When a vertex at distance <code>k</code> is removed, newly discovered neighbors get distance <code>k+1</code>.</p><p>The queue processes layers in nondecreasing distance order. If a newly discovered neighbor had a shorter path, its previous layer would already have discovered it, a contradiction. Thus first discovery establishes a shortest <strong>unweighted hop count</strong>. Marking at enqueue ensures each reachable vertex enters the queue once, including cycles and self-loops.</p><p>The code uses a head index instead of repeated <code>shift()</code>. It retains the consumed queue entries for this one run. Validation plus traversal costs O(V+E) under expected constant-cost Map/Set operations and bounded IDs; queue, distances and output each use O(V) entries. Reachable-component work alone is O(Vr+Er), but the full validator still examines all V/E.</p><h2 id="dfs-frame-invariant" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">4 </span>DFS frame invariant <a class="header-anchor" href="#dfs-frame-invariant" aria-label="Permalink to “4 DFS frame invariant”">​</a></h2><p>Each explicit frame stores a vertex and the index of its next unexamined neighbor. Frames describe the current active path. A newly discovered vertex is marked before pushing its frame; preorder records that discovery. When a frame has no neighbors left, postorder records its finish and the frame pops.</p><p>The saved index prevents rescanning completed edges after returning from a child. Each reachable vertex is pushed/popped once and each outgoing edge examined once. The frontier stack is at most O(V), plus O(V) visited/output. Validation still contributes O(V+E).</p><p>This is a reachability DFS. A single visited set cannot distinguish a gray active ancestor from a black finished vertex. To detect directed cycles use active/finished state; to compute a topological order first establish the graph is acyclic. Reversing this code&#39;s postorder does not magically topologically sort the cyclic fixture. Those extensions are optional.</p><h2 id="inorder-and-tree-correctness" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">5 </span>Inorder and tree correctness <a class="header-anchor" href="#inorder-and-tree-correctness" aria-label="Permalink to “5 Inorder and tree correctness”">​</a></h2><p>A binary node has <code>value</code>, <code>left</code> and <code>right</code>. Inorder descends left, emits the node, then visits right. The explicit stack holds ancestors waiting for that emission/right subtree; after finishing a left subtree, popping the next ancestor is exactly the next inorder step.</p><p>For root 2 with children 1 and 3, output is <code>[1,2,3]</code>. For root 1 with left 3 and right 2 it is <code>[3,1,2]</code>. <strong>Inorder produces sorted values only for a valid BST</strong>, not for any binary tree. A BST must specify its duplicate-key policy; “all left smaller, all right larger” excludes duplicates, while a one-sided duplicate convention changes that rule.</p><p>The retained source rejects nonfinite values, more than 200 nodes, cycles and shared child identities. Unknown JSON still needs shape validation; TypeScript declarations do not parse it. For an already validated proper tree, the traversal stack uses O(h), where height is O(log n) when balanced and O(n) for a chain. This implementation also keeps an O(n) validation set and O(n) output, so quoting only O(h) total auxiliary memory is wrong.</p><h2 id="scope-and-failure-boundaries" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">6 </span>Scope and failure boundaries <a class="header-anchor" href="#scope-and-failure-boundaries" aria-label="Permalink to “6 Scope and failure boundaries”">​</a></h2><p><a href="./../workbench/cs-foundations/traversals.html">Canonical traversal source</a> retains the original Big O page&#39;s implementations unchanged. Proper inputs are application-owned and unchanged during traversal; readonly types do not freeze objects. Empty graph/start mismatch rejects; a null tree returns <code>[]</code>. Graph cycles are allowed; tree cycles/shared children reject because their contracts differ.</p><p>These synchronous bounded functions do not implement cancellation, CPU isolation or arbitrary weighted shortest paths. Large jobs need separate work/resource policies from the <a href="./../node-js/">Node runtime course</a>; the data structure alone does not provide them.</p><p><a href="./../practice/cs-foundations/dependency-explorer.html#cs-p05-note-dependency-explorer">Separate dependency-explorer exercise</a> · <a href="./priority-queues.html">Next: priority scheduling</a></p><p>Primary terminology references: <a href="https://www.boost.org/doc/libs/latest/libs/graph/doc/html/graph/algorithms/traversal/traversal_overview.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Boost traversal overview</a>, <a href="https://xlinux.nist.gov/dads/HTML/inorderTraversal.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NIST inorder</a>. Correctness/cost reasoning above is for the retained source and its explicit validation policy.</p>',20))])}const f=a(d,[["render",c]]);export{m as __pageData,f as default};
